record legeos.observation.home-hands-on-failures-diagnosed/1 : observation {
    title "Why the Home Field failed at a real desktop: four typing defects, an inverted rung rule, an unregistered interface and a mixed-age install"
    state verified
    scope [
        path "apps/lege-greeter/**",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/**",
        path "services/lege-sessiond/**",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    statement """
        A desktop tester reported that typing was slow and error-prone, that there was no logout control, and that the shutdown control was always visible and did nothing. Every cause was located and none was a regression; all were present in a workspace-green tree.

        Typing, four independent defects. (1) Both winit surfaces translated a key press with `.chars().next()`, but `Key::Character` carries a `SmolStr` because a dead-key or compose commit with no precomposed form arrives as base letter plus combining mark in one event — the remainder was dropped silently (shell/lege-shell/src/surface/window.rs:125 and the identical copy in apps/lege-greeter/src/surface/window.rs, where the field is a password). (2) The compositor asked for `XkbConfig::default()` — empty rules/model/layout/variant and `options: None` — so no configured layout was honoured and no Compose key existed (compositor/lege-comp/src/state.rs:205). (3) `ensure_launcher_icons` ran on the winit event-loop thread on every redraw, and every accepted key requests a redraw: each miss read .desktop files across every XDG_DATA_DIRS entry, stat-ed a candidate per size bucket per extension per theme directory, then decoded a PNG or rasterized an SVG at 128x128, between a key press and the frame showing its character (shell/lege-shell/src/surface/app.rs:501, shell/lege-shell/src/icons.rs:241-367). (4) Full repaint and full-buffer present per keystroke with no damage tracking, which sets the latency floor the third defect then exceeds. `pixelkit_shell::Input` holds `keys` as a Vec, so no key is dropped by queueing.

        Power controls. The session widget declared `session.lock` and `session.poweroff` at `Representation::Small` and `session.logout` at `Wide` (shell/lege-shell/src/surface/widgets/mod.rs:102-108), while a system tile is created at its floor span and stays there (shell/lege-shell/src/field_model.rs:227) — so on every real desktop the destructive action was the one that could not be hidden and the safe one was behind a resize the default density makes inert. Separately, `Session.Lifecycle1` was registered only if the system-bus connection had succeeded at sessiond startup, with no retry, unlike `Location1` two lines above (services/lege-sessiond/src/main.rs:388-409, 552-564): losing that race removed every power control for the whole session. Every failure path in the shell's `spawn_lifecycle` was a `tracing::error!` and nothing on screen, and `draw_region_strip` never consulted logind's Can* verdicts, so a control known to be refused was drawn identically to a working one.

        Install coherence. The installed set was mixed-age: ten of thirteen LegeOS packages were more than a day older than lege-shell, so a 2026-08-25 shell was calling interfaces and protocol requests that the 2026-08-22 sessiond and 2026-08-23 compositor predate.
        """
    observed_at git:a185a0043a662c2923503984557948f598ee5220
    method manual
    watches [
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/window.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/app.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/widgets/mod.rs",
        "compositor/lege-comp/src/state.rs",
        "services/lege-sessiond/src/main.rs"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.home-widget-foundations ]
}

record legeos.observation.home-refresh-costs-profiled-20260902/1 : observation {
    title "Home's three-second refresh profiled: what was fixed, and the two service-side costs that remain"
    state verified
    scope [
        path "services/lege-sessiond/src/apis/audio.rs",
        path "services/lege-sessiond/src/apis/lifecycle.rs",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    statement """
        Measured against a live sessiond on the development host, one pass of the Home Surface's three-second refresh loop cost about 82ms, of which: fetch_apps 34ms, fetch_settings_widget 19ms, fetch_session_widget 18ms, fetch_all(20) 5.5ms, fetch_projects 2.6ms, fetch_browser_sources 0.8ms. Separately, zbus's Connection::session() builds a new connection — socket, SASL handshake, Hello and a reader task — on every call, and the shell called it from three dozen places; caching one connection per tokio runtime took fetch_all from 14ms to 5.5ms.

        Fixed in 392bd0d and f148046: the connection cache, joining the loop's independent fetches, joining the Session widget's three logind Can* questions (18ms to 13ms), and moving the application-catalogue read onto AppIdentity1.ApplicationsChanged plus a thirty-second backstop. An ordinary tick is now about 27ms.

        Two costs remain and both are inside lege-sessiond, not the shell. Audio1.Get spawns `wpctl` as a child process on every call, which is most of fetch_settings_widget's remaining time; the Home Settings widget therefore spawns a process every three seconds for as long as the session lasts. Lifecycle1's Can* answers still cost about 13ms even when the three are asked together, which suggests the system-bus round trips to logind are being serialised inside sessiond rather than by the caller; logind's answer to whether this machine may shut down does not change between two three-second ticks and is a candidate for caching.
        """
    observed_at git:392bd0d8a20b8ad0b43311cfde9ad58543c942a6
    method command
    watches [
        "shell/lege-shell/src/data.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/app.rs",
        "services/lege-sessiond/src/apis/audio.rs",
        "services/lege-sessiond/src/apis/lifecycle.rs"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.home-field ]
}

record legeos.observation.home-tree-exceeds-the-inotify-watch-budget/1 : observation {
    title "The home tree needs half again more inotify watches than the index's budget allows, so watcher exhaustion is the normal outcome rather than an edge case"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/lege-file-index/src/watch.rs" ]
    statement """
        Measured on the reference laptop (kernel 6.17) on 2026-09-04: $HOME holds 230,323 directories, and 189,089 after applying the index's own skip list (189,087 with a depth<=24 clamp, so depth is not a factor). The project drive /mnt/Samsung980_1TB adds a further 172,681 directories. fs.inotify.max_user_watches is 250,074 and WatchBudget::from_limits deliberately takes half of it, giving 125,037. A single home root therefore requires about 1.5x the budget the index allows itself, and a full drive index would require about 1.6x the entire per-user kernel allowance. The consequence is that arm_recursive refuses watches past the budget, marks the pass incomplete and stops descending, so the root commits as Stale{WatcherExhausted} on every reconcile: the WatcherExhausted fallback path is the steady state on this machine, not an exceptional condition. This also contradicts the figure of 157,283 post-skip directories recorded in crates/lege-file-index/src/watch.rs and .agent/scratch/de01/F2-plan.md on 2026-09-03; the discrepancy is not explained by depth clamping and is left for the benchmark lane to settle from the index's own policy. Both figures exceed the budget, so the conclusion holds either way.
        """
    observed_at git:be57fadad93f5918c55ebe3c216807fa9e7ff73c
    method command
    watches [ "crates/lege-file-index/src/watch.rs" ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.file-index-maintenance ]
}

record legeos.observation.index-baseline-2026-09-04/1 : observation {
    title "Index baseline measurements (2026-09-04): watch-budget shortfall confirmed, idle-wakeup fix confirmed at 0"
    state verified
    statement """
        Measured on the reference laptop (kernel 6.17, ext4) via `cargo run -q -p xtask -- index-bench`: $HOME holds 189,094 post-skip directories (RootPolicy::default_home), 1.51x WatchBudget::max_watches (125,037) and 0.76x the whole-kernel fs.inotify.max_user_watches allowance (250,074) — confirming a home root cannot stay fully watched under inotify on this machine by construction. The stale 157,283 figure previously in watch.rs's doc comment (recorded 2026-09-03) was simply out of date, not a measurement-methodology difference (find vs RootPolicy and depth-clamping are both ruled out); corrected in commit 6c6a79b. Idle-cost headline number measured as a real before/after pair across commit 6522e10 (an idle index watcher sleeps rather than wakes twice a second): watcher-thread schedstat pcount and voluntary_ctxt_switches over a 60s idle window went from 121/120 (pre-fix, built from worktree at be57fad) to 0/0 (post-fix, HEAD). 100k-entry workload suite: initial index 28,389 entries/s, warm 100-result search p50 0.96ms/p95 1.27ms, snapshot of 90,152 entries in 1.28s (12.2MB). 2,000,000-entry case not run (harness supports it via --allow-two-million --release; left for a follow-up). Full detail: docs/measurements/index-baseline-2026-09-04.md.
        """
    observed_at git:6c6a79b617b1e387f5ae64da9bbee54a021e30a5
    method command
    depends_on [ @legeos.work.adaptive-index-maintenance ]
}

record legeos.observation.index-change-signals-never-fire/1 : observation {
    title "Index1's GenerationAdvanced and RootStateChanged signals are implemented but never wired, so no client can observe the index changing"
    state verified
    scope [
        path "services/lege-filed/src/index_host.rs",
        path "services/lege-filed/src/main.rs"
    ]
    statement """
        Index1Signals in services/lege-filed/src/index_host.rs implements index_core::ChangeSink and can emit both GenerationAdvanced and RootStateChanged, but nothing calls Index::set_change_sink (crates/lege-file-index/src/lib.rs) in this build; its own doc comment records the gap. Both signals are therefore dead: a client watching the index for changes receives nothing, and every consumer must poll. This blocks the adaptive-observer design, whose CatchingUp/Resuming step depends on telling an arriving consumer that a fresher generation has committed. A second defect is latent in the same type: Index1Signals calls bare tokio::spawn, but ChangeSink is a synchronous trait invoked from the plain std::thread watcher loop (services/lege-filed/src/watcher.rs), and tokio::spawn panics when called outside a runtime context. Wiring the sink without also capturing a tokio::runtime::Handle would therefore panic on the first mutation, unwind the watcher thread, and leave the daemon running but permanently blind — a worse failure than the dead signal it replaces.
        """
    observed_at git:be57fadad93f5918c55ebe3c216807fa9e7ff73c
    method observation
    watches [ "services/lege-filed/src/index_host.rs", "services/lege-filed/src/main.rs" ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.file-index-maintenance ]
}

record legeos.observation.lege-client-links-rusqlite-through-the-shared-index-types/1 : observation {
    title "The index consumer library links rusqlite transitively, so a dependency check cannot stand in for the no-shared-store rule"
    state verified
    statement """
        `cargo tree -p lege-client -i rusqlite` succeeds: lege-client depends on lege-file-index, which depends on rusqlite. The link is real but it is a *linkage*, not a store access — `crates/lege-client/src/tree_source.rs` uses only the crate's `model`, `query` and `snapshot` types, and no consumer path opens a database. Separating those types from the store means feature-gating rusqlite out of lege-file-index and editing `crates/lege-client/Cargo.toml`, `cli/lege/Cargo.toml`, `crates/lege-fanotify/Cargo.toml` and `tools/xtask/Cargo.toml`, which this campaign did not do. So the `file-index-contract` acceptance check is satisfied by `crates/lege-file-index/tests/consumer_contract.rs` — which drives every read through snapshot and `changes_since` and never through `Index::open` — and not by a dependency-graph command that would have to be reported as failing when it passes.
        """
    observed_at git:eb00239b41cfcb615f01b34bdb2903ed16f4f51f
    method command
    watches [ "crates/lege-client/Cargo.toml", "crates/lege-file-index/Cargo.toml" ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.decision.event-driven-service-authorities ]
}

record legeos.observation.openuri-is-the-frontends-portal/1 : observation {
    title "There is no OpenURI backend interface to implement: the portal frontend owns OpenURI, and launches the handler outside LegeOS identity"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/lege-associationd/**", path "portals/xdg-desktop-portal-lege/**" ]
    statement """
        `org.freedesktop.impl.portal.OpenURI` does not exist. On the declared Ubuntu base, `xdg-desktop-portal 1.20.3+ds-1ubuntu1.1` knows 25 impl interfaces and OpenURI is not among them (`strings /usr/libexec/xdg-desktop-portal | grep 'impl.portal'`); no `/usr/share/dbus-1/interfaces/*OpenURI*` XML is installed; and none of the six installed backends (gtk, kde, cosmic, lxqt, kwallet, gnome-keyring) claims it. The frontend implements `org.freedesktop.portal.OpenURI` itself: it finds the default handler through GAppInfo and `mimeapps.list`, delegates only the 'which application' question to `org.freedesktop.impl.portal.AppChooser`, and then launches the chosen desktop entry in its own scope.

        Two consequences for LegeOS, both live at this commit. First, the launch does not go through `Launcher1`, so a file opened from a browser is not started into a LegeOS cgroup; since identity comes from the cgroup by design, that process is anonymous to `AppIdentity1` and to every policy keyed on it. Second, `lege-associationd` regenerates its `mimeapps.list` export after every association mutation (`crates/lege-associationd/src/dbus.rs`) but nothing writes it at session start (`services/lege-sessiond/src/main.rs` wires `AssociationApi` without an initial export), so a fresh home — or one whose file was removed — can disagree with `Associations1` until something changes a default.

        The portal backend was corrected to match this reality rather than to claim an OpenURI interface nothing would call: `legeos.portal` still claims exactly four interfaces, and the AppChooser leg was fixed to recognise a LegeOS canonical app id among the desktop file ids the frontend offers (`AppIdentity1.ListApplications`), which is what makes the LegeOS default actually win.
        """
    observed_at git:8b284c9147b70b4f03bb7c48ec993647e2e4df31
    method observation
    watches [
        "portals/xdg-desktop-portal-lege/**",
        "crates/lege-associationd/**",
        "services/lege-sessiond/src/main.rs"
    ]
}

record legeos.observation.reconciliation-cost-tracks-tree-size-not-change-size/1 : observation {
    title "A reconcile that finds nothing changed costs as much as one that finds a hundred changes, and both scale with the tree"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "crates/lege-file-index/src/mutation.rs",
        path "crates/lege-file-index/src/reconcile.rs"
    ]
    statement """
        Measured on the development host (ext4, NVMe, warm page cache so read_bytes was zero and these are best-case figures). Recursive reconciliation with the change set held fixed and the tree grown: 1,000 files / 1,539 entries took 173 ms with nothing changed and 127 ms with 100 files changed; 10,000 files / 15,338 entries took 2,179 ms and 4,197 ms; 100,000 files / 153,326 entries took 39,594 ms with nothing changed and 33,440 ms with 100 files changed. Crawl cost is therefore entirely proportional to tree size and essentially insensitive to how much actually changed, which is the property that makes it unsuitable as steady-state maintenance. By contrast Index::apply is batch-proportional: against the same 153,326-entry index, a 100-mutation batch took 64 ms and a 1,000-mutation batch 133 ms, roughly 250x cheaper than the equivalent crawl before any delta source is added. A separate finding fell out of the same run: a repeated single-mutation batch still costs about 51 ms at 153,326 entries against about 8 ms at 15,338, almost entirely CPU, because apply_transaction recomputes entry_count with a full COUNT(*) over every live row of the root on every commit (mutation.rs:310-316). That is roughly 0.33 microseconds per entry, extrapolating to about 330 ms per batch on a million-entry home, and the watcher pays it on every coalesced batch.
        """
    observed_at git:be57fadad93f5918c55ebe3c216807fa9e7ff73c
    method instrumented
    watches [
        "crates/lege-file-index/src/reconcile.rs",
        "crates/lege-file-index/src/mutation.rs"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.adaptive-index-maintenance ]
}

record legeos.observation.reconciliation-cost-tracks-tree-size-not-change-size/2 : observation {
    title "A reconcile that finds nothing changed costs as much as one that finds a hundred changes, and both scale with the tree"
    state verified
    scope [
        path "crates/lege-file-index/src/mutation.rs",
        path "crates/lege-file-index/src/reconcile.rs"
    ]
    statement """
        Re-measured on a quiet machine at 2026-09-04 13:25, after every lane had stopped compiling. Recursive reconciliation with the change set held fixed and the tree grown, release build, warm page cache (read_bytes zero throughout, so these are best-case): 1,000 files / 1,540 entries took 16 ms to acquire, 21 ms to reconcile with nothing changed and 26 ms with 100 files changed; 10,000 files / 15,339 entries took 157 / 281 / 254 ms; 100,000 files / 153,327 entries took 1,867 ms to acquire, 3,240 ms with nothing changed and 3,375 ms with 100 files changed. The architectural conclusion is unchanged and is the point of the record: reconcile cost is proportional to tree size and essentially blind to how much actually changed, so it cannot serve as steady-state maintenance. By contrast Index::apply is now flat in tree size after the accelerator-index work: a warm single-mutation batch costs 0.2 ms at 15,339 entries and 0.3 ms at 153,327, against 2.7 ms and 41.9 ms before, and a 100-mutation batch at 153,327 entries costs 4.1 ms against 43.1 ms before. A crawl therefore costs roughly 790x a hundred-mutation batch at this size. This revision corrects revision 1, which recorded 32,838 ms acquisition and 39,594 ms no-change reconcile at 153,326 entries: those figures were about ten times too high because they were taken while four other lanes were compiling on the same machine. Removing the new accelerator index leaves the reconcile at 2,027 / 3,420 ms, confirming the correction is a quieter machine rather than the index work. Any figure in docs/measurements/btrfs-delta-2026-09-04.md derived from the original run is wrong by roughly an order of magnitude and should be read against this record.
        """
    observed_at git:be57fadad93f5918c55ebe3c216807fa9e7ff73c
    method instrumented
    watches [
        "crates/lege-file-index/src/reconcile.rs",
        "crates/lege-file-index/src/mutation.rs"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.adaptive-index-maintenance ]
    supersedes [ @legeos.observation.reconciliation-cost-tracks-tree-size-not-change-size/1 ]
}

record legeos.observation.release-debs-ship-unstripped-line-tables/1 : observation {
    title "Every shipped binary carries its line tables: the debs are ~5x their stripped size, deliberately, and the ISO will inherit that"
    state verified
    scope [ path "Cargo.toml", path "distro/iso/**", path "distro/metapackages/**" ]
    statement """
        `[profile.release]` sets `debug = "line-tables-only"` (Cargo.toml:139, present since the initial commit) so a panic backtrace off a real desktop names file:line rather than a hex address. `cargo deb` is told neither to strip nor to split symbols, so the byte-identical unstripped binary goes into the package: lege-hostd is 22.7 MB shipped against 4.5 MB stripped, and every deb in the set carries the same multiple. This is the accepted trade for the developer edition, not an oversight — the point is that a live ISO built from this archive will be several hundred MB larger than the same set stripped, and that is the expected size, not a fault to chase. Turning it off later is `separate-debug-symbols = true` under each `[package.metadata.deb]` (thirteen one-line additions), which keeps the line tables in a sidecar and ships the stripped binary; do that when medium size starts to matter, and expect the ISO to shrink sharply.
        """
    observed_at git:9855da2f2647f2baa7473b2dda60b20dd702a046
    method command
    watches [ "Cargo.toml", "*/*/Cargo.toml", "distro/iso/**" ]
    blocks [ @legeos.work.self-hosting-legeos ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.decision.debian-packaging ]
}

record legeos.observation.shelved-tiles-have-no-route-back/1 : observation {
    title "A stashed tile has no route back onto the field, and a drawer already sits on the shelf unreachable"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs", path "shell/lege-shell/**" ]
    statement """
        The action manifest declares `field.stash` but no inverse. Delete shelves the focused tile, `lege_field` keeps it in `tiles` with no location, and the only way back is `field.undo` immediately afterwards — nothing enumerates the shelf, and no action, context-menu entry or chord restores from it. `FieldDocument::shelved()` exists and has no user-facing surface above it.

        This is not hypothetical on the developer host: the saved field carries the Recent drawer on the shelf, which is why Recent renders nowhere. `merge_drawers` cannot bring it back, because it skips any drawer whose tile already exists and correctly refuses to undo a deliberate stash — a tile shelved by accident and one shelved on purpose are indistinguishable once saved.

        The Window Shelf drawer covers minimised windows only. There is no equivalent for tiles.
        """
    observed_at git:53f7fd81e0e68bdff94978d180304fd0935509b6
    method observation
    watches [
        "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/context_menu.rs"
    ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.evidence.home-field-reshape-across-resolutions-20260831 ]
}

record legeos.observation.shelved-tiles-have-no-route-back/2 : observation {
    title "A stashed tile has no route back onto the field, and a drawer already sits on the shelf unreachable"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs", path "shell/lege-shell/**" ]
    statement """
        Resolved by @legeos.work.home-shelf-and-representation-ladder: `field.restore` brings every shelved tile back, and `field.reset` discards a stored layout so the current default is adopted. Kept for the record because it is why both exist.

        As observed: the action manifest declared `field.stash` with no inverse. Delete shelved the focused tile, `lege_field` kept it in `tiles` with no location, and the only way back was `field.undo` immediately afterwards — nothing enumerated the shelf, and no action, context-menu entry or chord restored from it. `FieldDocument::shelved()` existed with no user-facing surface above it.

        This was not hypothetical on the developer host: the saved field carried the Recent drawer on the shelf, which is why Recent rendered nowhere. `merge_drawers` could not bring it back, because it skips any drawer whose tile already exists and correctly refuses to undo a deliberate stash — a tile shelved by accident and one shelved on purpose are indistinguishable once saved.
        """
    observed_at git:53f7fd81e0e68bdff94978d180304fd0935509b6
    method observation
    watches [
        "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs",
        "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/context_menu.rs"
    ]
    depends_on [ @legeos.work.home-shelf-and-representation-ladder ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.evidence.home-field-reshape-across-resolutions-20260831 ]
    supersedes [ @legeos.observation.shelved-tiles-have-no-route-back/1 ]
}

record legeos.observation.the-image-drop-in-for-a-named-build-set-already-exists/1 : observation {
    title "compose.sh already writes the mkosi drop-in that points the image at a named build set"
    state verified
    statement """
        `distro/image/compose.sh:22` writes `distro/image/mkosi.conf.d/40-local-archive.conf` from `$LEGEOS_BUILD_SET`, falling back to the archive's `current` marker and writing no drop-in at all when neither exists — which is the documented mkosi drop-in D2's `image-drop-in` check asks for. It cannot be exercised here: mkosi is not installed on this host, so `just image summary` is NOT EXECUTED (@legeos.evidence.de01-image-summary-mkosi-absent-20260904), and the check's own wording allows exactly that — 'or the drop-in is documented as not exercised because mkosi is absent'.
        """
    observed_at git:eb00239b41cfcb615f01b34bdb2903ed16f4f51f
    method observation
    watches [ "distro/image/compose.sh", "distro/image/mkosi.conf.d/**" ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.decision.build-set-id-is-the-version-authority ]
}

record legeos.observation.the-provisioner-wrote-no-fstab-at-all/1 : observation {
    title "A provisioned machine had no /etc/fstab, and its boot identity was a label every LegeOS disk shares"
    state verified
    statement """
        `installer/provision.sh` never wrote `/etc/fstab`: the word does not appear anywhere under `installer/`, `distro/` or `session/`. The mounts it makes are for the chroot and were never recorded, so a provisioned machine had nothing telling it how to mount @home, @var, @snapshots, @deployments or the ESP on its own. Separately, boot identity was `root=LABEL=legeos-root`, hardcoded in `boot_entries.rs:43` and `deploy.rs:128` — the same string on every LegeOS disk, so a machine with two attached mounts whichever the kernel enumerated first. This was scoped as a label-to-UUID migration; it was a missing step plus a migration.
        """
    observed_at git:eb00239b41cfcb615f01b34bdb2903ed16f4f51f
    method observation
    watches [ "installer/provision.sh", "services/lege-hostd/src/boot_entries.rs" ]
    derived_from [ @legeos.decision.independent-storage-roles ]
}

record legeos.observation.this-machines-partition-table-cannot-hold-legeos-as-it-stands/1 : observation {
    title "The reference laptop has one 55.9 GiB free tail, a 100 MiB ESP, and a provisioner that only takes a whole disk"
    state verified
    scope [ path "distro/**", path "installer/**" ]
    statement """
        Measured on the reference laptop. nvme0n1 (953.9 GiB) holds NTFS 536G, NTFS 949M, Lubuntu's ext4 root 416G with 8.7G free, the 100 MiB ESP and a 16 MiB partition, leaving a 0.5 GiB free tail. nvme1n1 (931.5 GiB) holds a 16 MiB partition and the 875.6 GiB NTFS projects partition, leaving 55.9 GiB unallocated at its tail. Three consequences. (1) The only place a LegeOS root can go without destroying Lubuntu or Windows is that 55.9 GiB tail, which fits a 1 GiB ESP and a ~54 GiB btrfs root but not a 42 GiB target directory. (2) distro/deploy/layout.toml requires an ESP of 512M-1G and the existing one is 100 MiB, sandwiched between Lubuntu's root and a 16 MiB partition, so a second ESP on nvme1n1 is the answer rather than growing it. (3) installer/provision.sh runs sgdisk --zap-all on its target and has no mode that consumes free space beside existing partitions, so it cannot be run on this machine at all as it stands. Separately: five Cargo path dependencies point outside the repository, so a LegeOS installation could not rebuild LegeOS; mmdebstrap and debootstrap are not installed on this host; and nm-tray-2 is neither packaged nor named in the seed declaration.
        """
    observed_at git:024c38e68614b4f9333a011242af1cac8d97c919
    method manual
    watches [
        "installer/provision.sh",
        "distro/deploy/layout.toml",
        "Cargo.toml",
        "distro/seeds/selection.toml"
    ]
}

record legeos.observation.transient-unit-launch-converged-independently/1 : observation {
    title "Omarchy launches applications into transient systemd units too, then identifies windows by self-declared app_id anyway"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "crates/lege-appd/**", path "crates/lege-types/**" ]
    statement """
        Basecamp's Omarchy independently arrived at the same launch primitive LegeOS uses: bin/omarchy-launch-browser and five sibling scripts start applications with `systemd-run --user --collect --unit="omarchy-browser-$(date +%s%N)"`, giving one transient unit and therefore one cgroup per launch. It then does not use it. There is no reference to a cgroup, a unit or /proc/<pid>/cgroup anywhere under shell/; the shell identifies windows purely from the client's self-declared app_id (12 references). The cgroup exists and the information is discarded. This sharpens what LegeOS's identity model actually contributes: not that a per-launch cgroup exists, but that naming it app-legeos-<AppId>@<InstanceId>.service makes the cgroup name carry the identity, so a window's PID resolves to a cgroup, the cgroup name resolves to an AppId, and the governor, the dock and the notification router all key off one fact nobody had to ask the client for. Omarchy's unit name is a timestamp and carries no identity, so it cannot make that journey even though it has the cgroup. Independent convergence on the primitive is evidence the primitive is right; the naming convention is the part that turns it into an identity system, and is the part to defend in review.
        """
    observed_at git:c0302dabc9dc3e0e0ba2c5d3a7c66d05ef0f5eb3
    method manual
    source {
        kind internal
        role evidence
        path "docs/research/omarchy-review.md"
        use """
            Section 7, correction 3 — verified against the cloned source rather than taken from the study's body, which had framed the difference as trusting app_id versus using cgroups.
            """
    }
}

record legeos.observation.transient-unit-launch-converged-independently/2 : observation {
