record legeos.observation.trash-undo-reported-a-restore-it-did-not-perform/1 : observation {
    title "Undoing a trash restored nothing and still committed — and the covering test hid it by substituting its own inputs"
    state verified
    scope [ path "services/lege-filed/**" ]
    statement """
        Undo of a trash operation reported success and moved no file. undo_of built the undo plan from the operation's own paths — the pre-trash locations, the only ones OperationKind::Trash retains — so plan emitted Step::RestoreFromTrash aimed at a path the file had just vacated; execute found nothing there, took that for 'already restored', returned Ok, and the operation reached Committed. Ctrl+Z in the Files app and lege files undo both told the user the file was back when it was still in the trash. The same defect existed in the other direction, undoing a restore, with no test coverage at all.

        What matters beyond the fix: the test that should have caught this concealed it. trashing_and_undoing_it_round_trip took undo_of's result, discarded its paths, looked the real trashed paths up itself via trash::list, and passed those in — a substitution production never performs. The test therefore exercised a path no caller takes and passed for four months while the production path was broken. A test that repairs its subject's output before asserting on it is not covering that subject; the repair is the bug, made invisible.

        Fixed at git:464e069b7b2769bc5e0ab34e8191b65f9101e7db by recording where the trash actually put each item into the executed step, the way Move's undo already reads its RenameInPlace destination. A post-hoc trash::list lookup could not serve: trash::free_name resolves collisions against the live directory, so a path trashed twice leaves two records and nothing outside the operation can say which is which. The corrected test feeds undo_of straight into plan; reverting the fix fails it.
        """
    observed_at git:464e069b7b2769bc5e0ab34e8191b65f9101e7db
    method command
    watches [
        "services/lege-filed/src/execute.rs",
        "services/lege-filed/src/operations.rs",
        "services/lege-filed/src/trash.rs"
    ]
}

record legeos.observation.two-daemons-opening-one-fresh-state-db-raced-its-migrations/1 : observation {
    title "lege-sessiond and lege-filed raced the first migration of the shared state database, so on a first login whichever started second did not start at all"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/lege-state/src/lib.rs", path "tests/integration/nested-session.sh" ]
    statement """
        Found by running the desktop-validation acceptance check `nested-session-journey` (tests/integration/nested-session.sh), which had never been run and did not pass. Two defects, one of them in production code. (1) The harness waited for the trusted-shell socket under the name `<socket>-shell` while the compositor creates it as `legeos-shell-<socket>` (compositor/lege-comp/src/shell_socket.rs:31). The prefix is deliberate — a trailing `-shell` on `wayland-0` falls inside snapd's `wayland-[0-9]*` permission, the one socket no confined application may open — and scripts/nested-session.sh carries that reasoning in a comment, but the integration harness was not updated when the naming changed on 2026-09-03. It could not have passed since. (2) The real one: `lege_state::migrate` checked whether a migration was applied with an unlocked read, then applied it inside a deferred `BEGIN`. Two processes opening a fresh database both read the migration table as empty and both ran the migration; the loser reached `CREATE TABLE instances` after the winner had committed one and died with `table instances already exists`. This is not a harness artefact: `$XDG_STATE_HOME/legeos/session.db` is one store per user by design and both `lege-sessiond` and `lege-filed` open it (services/lege-filed/src/main.rs defaults to the same path), systemd starts them together, and the window is exactly a first login on a machine with no database yet — so the failure is invisible in development and lands on the first user of a fresh install. Reproduced deterministically with four threads opening one fresh store: three of four failed, on `0002-session-domains` as well as `0001-initial`, one of them with `database is locked` rather than a duplicate table. Fixed with `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` and the applied-check repeated inside the transaction, which makes check-and-apply atomic; a deferred transaction that tries to upgrade to a write after another writer has committed returns SQLITE_BUSY immediately and does not honour a busy timeout, so the timeout alone would not have been enough. A `busy_timeout` of 5 s was added as well, because two daemons sharing one WAL database had none and would meet SQLITE_BUSY on ordinary concurrent writes too. Regression test: `a_fresh_store_survives_several_daemons_opening_it_at_once`.
        """
    observed_at git:8d74542b146fa8399817bd377094157b7bd1bb97
    method instrumented
    watches [
        "crates/lege-state/src/lib.rs",
        "tests/integration/nested-session.sh",
        "services/lege-filed/src/main.rs"
    ]
}

record legeos.observation.unprivileged-fanotify-costs-more-per-directory-than-inotify/1 : observation {
    title "Unprivileged fanotify is one mark per directory, arms 2.5x slower than inotify and costs about twice the memory, for 6.4% more headroom"
    state verified
    scope [ path "crates/lege-fanotify/**", path "crates/lege-file-index/src/watch.rs" ]
    statement """
        Measured on the reference laptop (kernel 6.17, ext4, uid 1000, no capabilities) over 100,000 directories, three runs, spread under 3%: inotify arms at 0.8-0.9 us per directory, fanotify inode marks at 2.1-2.2 us. Kernel SUnreclaim per directory is 104-106 B for inotify and 78-81 B for fanotify; process VmRSS per directory is 68 B for inotify and 287 B for fanotify, because every fanotify event in fid mode names its directory by opaque file handle and open_by_handle_at(2) is EPERM without CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH, so the handle-to-path map must live in userspace at one entry per directory. Totals: ~172 B per directory for inotify against ~366 B for fanotify. Against the kernel ceiling, 189,089 directories are 75.6% of fs.inotify.max_user_watches (250,074) and 71.1% of fs.fanotify.max_user_marks (266,105) — 6.4% more headroom where a home root needs 51% more than the index's WatchBudget. On throughput the two are indistinguishable: identical raw event counts and kinds for a create+write workload (40,000 events from 40,000 operations) and for a cargo-build-shaped create+write+rename+delete burst (100,000 events from 80,000 operations), within 2% on delivery rate, and both overflow at exactly 16,385 events with nothing reading the fd. Unprivileged fanotify also requires a fid-reporting mode: FAN_CLASS_NOTIF with no fid flags, FAN_UNLIMITED_QUEUE, FAN_MARK_MOUNT and FAN_MARK_FILESYSTEM are all EPERM.
        """
    observed_at git:55de10d137980fb264a997f3f38c0f1349629944
    method command
    watches [ "crates/lege-fanotify/**", "crates/lege-file-index/src/watch.rs" ]
}
